  "worked_solution": "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{6-5}{-1-(-5)}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(-5,5).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-(-5)) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, we first multiply by $4$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+5) \\\\ 4\\cdot (y-5) & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(x+5) \\\\ 4y-20 & =x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-20 & =x+5 \\\\ 4y & =x+25 \\\\ -x+4y & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+4y & =25 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-x+4y) & =(-1)\\cdot 25 \\\\ x-4y & =-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $x-4y=-25.$",
